key members of Congress are reacting to  the America first agenda the president  outlined in his speech to Congress the  chorus became an earthquake and the  people turned out by the tens of  millions and they were all united by one  very simple but crucial demand that  America must put its own citizens first  because only then can we truly make  America great again Republican Senator  Marco Rubio of Florida was inside the  house chamber last night for the  President's speech and he joins us now  from Capitol Hill good morning senator  good morning good morning let's start if  we can with the president's comments on  immigration mr. Trump said that he saw  the possibility of immigration reform as  long as American jobs and security come  first you've been part of a bipartisan  effort for immigration reform in the  past that that did not work out do you  think this could be successful at this  time now I do it's not gonna be easy but  I believe that and by the way that's the  what our immigration law should always  be about obviously number one is  ensuring the people that are entering  the United States whether permanently or  as visitors or people that are not going  to harm us and second that ensures that  it's good for our country and our  economy I think how you do it is going  to be the difficult part and what I've  encouraged the president and others is  to view this as a multi-step process  it begins with enforcing our laws so  that we can win the confidence of the  American people the second step would be  to modernize our system make it you know  less bureaucratic and and more  merit-based and the third is to deal  reasonably but responsibly with people  that are here that have been here a long  time who are not criminals who are in  violation of our immigration laws but  but who we should try to accommodate for  both humanitarian and economic purposes  I believe if we follow those steps and  in that order we can achieve a result  that's good for the country the  president made it clear that he intends  to repeal and replace Obamacare in your  state of Florida you have a the largest  number of residents on Obama care in the  country do his views reflect your own  about the changes he intends to make  well I absolutely believe Obamacare  needs to be repealed because a lot of  those people that are on Obama care and  I'm meeting with hospitals today about  this are truly uninsured  even though they have an Obamacare  coverage their premiums are sorry their  deductibles are so high you know that in  essence for them they'd have to spend  money they don't have before they can  even begin to use the plan now that  doesn't mean you throw those people out  in the street it doesn't mean we go back  to the system we had before 2010 I don't  want to go back to that either I want to  replace it with a system that for the  vast majority Americans means they're  going to be able to access private  health insurance that's more affordable  and covers the things they want in a  vibrant private marketplace did you like  what the president had to say last night  senator about that well he outlined  broad principles I picked the detail and  I think there's a great agreement over  those principles I think the the  difficult work is laying out exactly how  we achieve that away from the system but  certainly the principles that we don't  want to disrupt people that are on it  now that we certainly don't want to go  back to the old system because that  wasn't working either yes I agree with  those principles and they are were  guiding us and it's what I've been  talking about for 40 since 2014 I've had  a plan to replace Obamacare and I'll  work hard to make sure as many of those  elements are part of our law as possible  senator we spoke with your colleague  Senator Chuck Schumer earlier this  morning and he said that presidents  President Trump's words and actions have  not been consistent that he has done  nothing to actually foster unity among  opposing parties is that true well I  think the that is in the eye of the  beholder there's no doubt this president  has an unorthodox style and there's no  doubt that he enters our politics as  president at a time of extraordinary  division I would say that that would be  true on both sides if you saw last night  there was a moment after this president  spoke were a significant number of the  members has got up and walked out before  he had even finished the speech or just  as he barely finished it I don't do that  stuff other people have to speak for  themselves there's something the  president's done that people think is  divisive they'll have to ask him about  it I will tell you this I do believe  that for the good of the country we need  to give him a chance to be successful  and if we disagree with them you know  we'll take on those issues but I thought  last night was a very presidential  speech I thought last night was the best  speech I've seen him give and I've been  watching him now pretty closely for two  years and I don't think there's any  doubt about that and he did a very good  job last night and that was a promising  sign senator the president again called  for a trillion dollar spending program  on infrastructure at the same time he  called for significant tax cuts for  businesses are there the votes in  in Congress for an infrastructure  spending program well it depends so if  the trillion dollars are all taxpayer  money then I think that's going to be  very difficult for the reasons we've  outlined however as he discussed last  night and I think a lot of people missed  this point he talked about a  public-private partnership and if  they're talking about leveraging private  funds as a significant portion of that  trillion dollars over a 10 or 5-year  period I think that is very doable  within the context of it but of  everything else but it's going to  require us to allow the federal  government to with its limited resources  to focus on the things the federal  government is in charge of doing and  that is the national security of our  country the interstate infrastructure  that's necessary for our nation and a  safety net that helps people who are  fallen on hard times to stand up and try  again and that's what we'll have to work  hard to ensure that we can get back to  and I believe President didn't talk  about this but I believe that eventually  we will have to deal sooner rather than  later with reforms to save Social  Security and Medicare well Congress has  its marching orders Senator Marco Rubio  thanks for your time thank you
 

